{"type":"standard","title":"Tropical cyclone naming","displaytitle":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tropical cyclone naming</span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q558163","titles":{"canonical":"Tropical_cyclone_naming","normalized":"Tropical cyclone naming","display":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tropical cyclone naming</span>"},"pageid":460919,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg/320px-Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg","width":320,"height":218},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Hurricane_Kate_%282003%29-_Good_pic.jpg","width":1000,"height":680},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1246814875","tid":"568d8347-77e8-11ef-b7fb-840fe6cd9b44","timestamp":"2024-09-21T07:08:44Z","description":"Tables of names for tropical cyclones","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone_naming","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone_naming?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone_naming?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tropical_cyclone_naming"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone_naming","revisions":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Tropical_cyclone_naming","edit":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone_naming?action=edit","talk":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tropical_cyclone_naming"}},"extract":"Tropical cyclones and subtropical cyclones are named by various warning centers to simplify communication between forecasters and the general public regarding forecasts, watches and warnings. The names are intended to reduce confusion in the event of concurrent storms in the same basin. Once storms develop sustained wind speeds of more than 33 knots, names are generally assigned to them from predetermined lists, depending on the basin in which they originate. Some tropical depressions are named in the Western Pacific, while tropical cyclones must contain a significant amount of gale-force winds before they are named in the Southern Hemisphere.","extract_html":"<p>Tropical cyclones and subtropical cyclones are named by various warning centers to simplify communication between forecasters and the general public regarding forecasts, watches and warnings. The names are intended to reduce confusion in the event of concurrent storms in the same basin. Once storms develop sustained wind speeds of more than 33 knots, names are generally assigned to them from predetermined lists, depending on the basin in which they originate. Some tropical depressions are named in the Western Pacific, while tropical cyclones must contain a significant amount of gale-force winds before they are named in the Southern Hemisphere.</p>"}